E=[('Father and mother, moved from Chapter 10', 101, '*which son am I looking at — and will I be the father, or one of the counterfeits?*', '*which son am I looking at — and will I be the father, or one of the counterfeits?*\n\nMost families know these two sons from the inside. A father often leans toward justice, and his temptation is to become the elder brother; a mother often leans toward mercy, and hers is to throw the party in the far country. This is part of how God made us. The Church teaches that the "difference and complementarity" of man and woman "are oriented toward the goods of marriage and the flourishing of family life" (*CCC* 2333). Each needs the other. His justice without her mercy crushes a child; her mercy without his justice spoils one. Held together, they begin to look like the father on the road, for in our Father in heaven, justice and mercy are one.'), ('The Mass: point back to Chapter 10', 230, 'And above all of these stands the Mass. Everything else on this list is fed there. The Mass is the source and summit of the whole Christian life (*CCC* 1324): in the Liturgy of the Word God instructs us, in the rhythm of her seasons He forms us, and in the Eucharist He does what no book or teacher ever could — He hands us Perfect Love itself, the very Body and Blood of Christ poured out, so that the love we are straining to learn becomes the food we receive.', 'And above all of these stands the Mass, the source and summit of the whole Christian life (*CCC* 1324), where, as we saw in Chapter 10, every part binds the four pillars together. Everything else on this list is fed there. In the Eucharist He does what no book or teacher ever could — He hands us Perfect Love itself, so that the love we are straining to learn becomes the food we receive.')]
